<!--
    The Swift convention of functions the metadata did not bind (`option
    swiftcall on|off`).

    Issue: the `swiftcall` arm bound `__swiftcall` only to functions Swift
    metadata places in a type. A closure's partial-apply forwarder kept the C
    model, so the context it reads from swiftself (r13 / x20) was an
    uninitialised local (`long v2; // r13`), and a two-word Swift result was a
    128-bit integer split by `SUB168(v1,0)` / `SUB168(v1,8)`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 prototype recovery. The decompile drive decodes the
    function and its direct callees (`p4_calls/kuna_swiftinfer.rs (seed)`); a
    body that reads swiftself on entry for anything but saving it decompiles
    under `__swiftcall` with the input named `swiftself`. The call-output type
    (`TypeOpCall::getOutputLocal`) of a two-word join whose words are read apart
    is `swift.pair`.

    The fixture `swift_infer_elf` is a stripped swiftc 6.3 build of
    `swift_infer_elf.swift` (recipe in the fixtures README): 0x24b0 is the
    partial-apply forwarder of `makeAdder`'s closure, 0x2840 is `usePair`, which
    reads both words `split` returns. The fixture `swift_pairret_elf` (recipe
    in the same README) holds a stripped `CustomStringConvertible.description`
    witness at 0x2140 that returns one string literal per path in rdx:rax: a
    function's own two-word result built by a `PIECE` of its words takes
    `swift.pair` as the return's input type (`TypeOpReturn::getInputLocal`,
    `pair_return_type`), where it was an `undefined16` returned through
    `*(unsigned __int128 *)v1`. Pass 1 (`option swiftcall off`) is the BUG,
    pass 2 (the default) the FIX.
-->
